exav-render: notices
====================

exav-render is licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE). This file
covers the office converters (the `office-sheets`, `office-slides` and
`office-docs` features, `src/formats/office/`). The vendored image decoders
keep their licences in `src/image_codecs/` and the stroke font its
dedication in `fonts/`; the notices of the rest of the workspace are in the
repository's NOTICE.

The office converters are written from published specifications and from the
black-box behaviour of other converters (their output on sample files). No
code of another office application is in them:

- no code of another spreadsheet application is in `sheets`;
- no code of another presentation application is in `slides`;
- no code of another word-processing application is in `docs`, and none was
  read while writing it;
- `common` is written from published specifications alone.

Specifications and documentation they follow
--------------------------------------------

Shared (`common`):

- Microsoft's Open Specifications: [MS-CFB], under the Microsoft Open
  Specifications Promise.
- OASIS OpenDocument 1.2 and 1.3, for the namespaces.
- Google's Snappy format description, and the protocol buffers encoding
  documented at protobuf.dev.

Spreadsheets (`sheets`):

- Microsoft's Open Specifications: [MS-XLS], [MS-OFFCRYPTO], [MS-ODRAW],
  [MS-CFB], under the Microsoft Open Specifications Promise.
- OpenOffice.org's "Microsoft Excel File Format" documentation (Daniel
  Rentz, Public Documentation License 1.0), for the BIFF2 to BIFF5 record
  layouts [MS-XLS] does not give, and the argument counts of the function
  table (exav-unpack's src/formats/biff/ftab.rs).
- OASIS OpenDocument 1.2 and 1.3, and OpenFormula.
- ECMA-376 (Office Open XML), for the XLSX written.
- Google's Snappy format description, and the protocol buffers encoding
  documented at protobuf.dev.
- Microsoft's "XML Spreadsheet Reference" (Office XP, 2001) for
  SpreadsheetML; the "Microsoft Office HTML and XML Reference" (Office
  2000) for Excel's HTML; Microsoft's "SYLK summary" (sylksum.doc, 1986)
  and knowledge base article Q64338 for SYLK, with ISO 6937 for its
  escapes; ECMA-376 for column widths in pixels; the W3C's CSS Color
  Module Level 4 for colour names.

Presentations (`slides`):

- Microsoft's Open Specifications: [MS-PPT], [MS-ODRAW], [MS-CFB],
  [MS-OSHARED] and [MS-OFFCRYPTO] (to recognise encrypted files), under the
  Microsoft Open Specifications Promise.
- ECMA-376 (Office Open XML) Part 1, for the PPTX written, and its
  presetShapeDefinitions.xml, for the preset shapes' names and adjustments.
- OASIS OpenDocument 1.2 (Part 1) and 1.3 (Part 3, schema).
- Google's Snappy format description, and the protocol buffers encoding
  documented at protobuf.dev.
- Apple's Technical Notes TN2067 (About the Keynote XML File Format, APXL
  Schema) and TN2073 (Deconstructing a Keynote Document: Part One,
  Slides), for Keynote 1 (src/formats/office/slides/keynote/apxl1.rs).
- RFC 1741 (BinHex 4.0), to find the data fork of a Macintosh PowerPoint
  file saved as text.
- Visio drawings (src/formats/office/slides/vsdx): Microsoft's Visio file
  format reference (Visio 2013 file format, learn.microsoft.com/office/
  client-developer/visio: the Introduction, Schema map, Elements and Types
  pages and the ShapeSheet reference pages of the geometry rows, the fill,
  line, character, paragraph, text block, layer and hyperlink cells, and the
  THEMEVAL and NURBS functions); [MS-VSDX] (Visio Graphics Service VSDX File
  Format), under the Microsoft Open Specifications Promise, for the package
  parts, the coordinate transforms, the inheritance of masters and styles,
  the colour table, the fill patterns, line patterns and line ends (pictures
  in it), the formula grammar and the theme; ECMA-376 Part 2 (Open
  Packaging Conventions), for the package and its relationships.

Text documents (`docs`):

- Microsoft's Open Specifications: [MS-DOC], [MS-ODRAW], [MS-OSHARED] and
  [MS-CFB], under the Microsoft Open Specifications Promise.
- ECMA-376 (Office Open XML) Part 1, for the DOCX written.
- OASIS OpenDocument 1.2 (Part 1) and 1.3 (Part 3, schema).
- Microsoft's Rich Text Format specification, version 1.9.1, and the
  WordprocessingML 2003 element and attribute names, for
  src/formats/office/docs/rtf and src/formats/office/docs/wml.
- Google's Snappy format description, and the protocol buffers encoding
  documented at protobuf.dev, for Pages (through `common`).

Formats nobody published
------------------------

- Microsoft published no description of PowerPoint 95 and of PowerPoint 2 to
  4, and Apple none of Keynote 2 to 5. src/formats/office/slides/ppt/ppt95.rs
  and src/formats/office/slides/keynote/apxl.rs were written from sample
  documents, the output of another office application on them used as a black
  box (rendered pages, never its source code), and the structure of the files themselves.
  src/formats/office/slides/ppt/old.rs reads only the headers of PowerPoint
  2 to 4, to report them as unsupported.

- Apple published none of Pages. src/formats/office/docs/pages was written
  from sample documents and the structure of the files themselves (the
  document message's page size and margin fields were found there), with the
  text messages' layout below.

- The Numbers '08 and '09 reader (src/formats/office/sheets/numbers/v09.rs)
  follows no description of the format: Apple published none. It was written
  from sample documents (Apache Tika's, below, and others used only as
  inputs), with another application's conversions of them as a black-box
  reference.

- The Visio reader (src/formats/office/slides/vsdx) is written from the
  specifications listed above and from sample drawings used only as inputs:
  48 VSDX files collected from public bug trackers and test suites of other
  projects (not part of this repository), to see what real files hold. Where
  the specifications are silent, the reader follows what those files and
  another application's rendering of them show, used as a black box (its PDF
  of each file compared with ours, never any source code, which was not
  read): the quick
  style colours 200 to 206 (read as 100 to 106), the side an arc's bow
  bulges to, and that a `Themed` value with no quick style of its own is the
  root style sheet's. The gradient patterns 25 to 40 are approximations of
  the pictures in [MS-VSDX]. Formats the reader leaves out: the binary .vsd
  and the Visio 2003 XML .vdx.

